model thyroid  and eggplant latent thyroid for example  have been assessed using the lethal  mutation method and reports have shown  anywhere from one in four  mutation rates ranging from one in four  hundred to one in a thousand nucleotides  these viroids are uh of the absence  absent viridae and they replicate in the  chloroplast using chloroplast rna  polymerases  previous estimates for pstbd  uh have ranged from one in  eight hundred to one in seven thousand  nucleotides in other words  around ten to the minus four one one  mistake and ten to the minus four  nucleotides suggesting that paul two  transcribes thyroid rna more accurately  than the chloroplast enzyme does  um but again these estimates were made  using the lethal mutation method and we  wish to uh we wish to assess  the pul2 error rate  and the psdvd mutation rate across the  entire genome in the absence of  selection  so this is our simple strategy for doing  that  in panel a you'll see a circular plus  strand genome with a red dot that read  that indicates a mutation so this is a  pre-existing mutation in a viroid genome  it's presumably a variant  and it's transcribed by paul ii in the  first in the first  uh  in the first instance to produce a minus  strand dimer  so that mutation should be present in  each of the monomers in other words it  should be shared by each of the monomers  again indicated by the red dots  however a mutation generated during that  transcription process  will not be shared by other monomers  that's the yellow dot  and we refer to those as unshared de  novo mutations those are generated by  paul ii during that transcription event  and so our strategy was to sequence  minus strand dimers and look for  unshared mutations  and the protocol is pretty  straightforward  extracts from the cochianabentamiana  plants this is a model tobacco that we  used as a host  we're infected with pstvd  cdna was prepared from the minus strand  concatemers using a reverse  transcriptase with a low error rate  and dimers were amplified from that cdna  by pcr using a very low error rate  polymerase for that purpose  and  we excise the dimers from the gel which  is shown in the middle  and subjected them to sanger sequencing  to avoid mutations generated during deep  sequencing  we did this in three independent  replicates indicated r a r b r c now  this is a lot of numbers on this table  but i'll talk you through it sort of  summarize things  each of these replicates had similar  numbers of dimers sequenced  including a control on the right  with the red asterisk  so in total  in the red boxes  we sequenced the total of 261 dimers  which is more than 500 genome  equivalents  and more than 187 000 total nucleotides  and in those 187 000 nucleotides we  found 102 unshared de novo mutations  so from that we could calculate an error  rate  of 1 in  1837  nucleotides  by contrast we found only one shared  mutation in all of these experiments  and the control  which we created  starting with a  cloned pstbd dimer we transcribed in  vitro with t3 rna polymerase we found  only one mutation so the background  mutation rate was negligible  now i'm going to go in a little more  detail into the paul 2 error rate and  mutation rate in the next slide  so again  we saw an apparent paul to error rate an  observed error rate if you will of 1 in  1837 nucleotides per transcription cycle  or  to put it another way an error rate of  5.4 times 10 to the minus 4.  from this we extrapolated a mutation  rate because we couldn't look at it  directly we extrapolate it  based on two transcription cycles and  assuming the same error rate for each  again assumptions but the best we could  do  to put it another way  twice the mutations in the same number  of nucleotide sequence  and we came up with an estimated  extrapolated mutation rate of about 1  and 10 to the minus 3 which turns out to  be very similar to the chloroplast  replicating thyroids  now  to think about the paul 2 error rate  again our initial question we're using a  non-native template here what's the paul  2 error rate for mrna transcription and  the best estimates if i could find  were from yeast and c elegans where they  agreed and both were about four times  ten to the minus six per base pair  so comparing the paul to error rate and  assuming similar transcription machinery  in the cochianabentamiana  we can say that paul 2 transcribes pstbd  rna with much lower fidelity than dna  templates about a hundred fold lower  fidelity  nevertheless oh these are some of the  unshared denoble mutations i got ahead  of myself there um that we found and  you'll see  they're listed for all three  replicates and again too many numbers to  look at but  about 30 percent of the dimers sequenced  actually contained an unshared mutation  most of them had only one  some had two or three  and all but one  involved a single base substitution  now some mutations appeared in multiple  genomes it turns out only 64 of those  102 mutations were actually unique  uh  some appeared in multiple genomes and in  many replicates and more than more than  one replicate and mutations observed in  more than one replicate are shown in red  okay  and some of them were found in all three  replicates  despite the low the the vast difference  in fidelity on different kinds of  templates the pul2 mutation spectrum on  dna and pstbdr in it  rna templates was actually quite similar  on the left you'll see transitions were  much much more likely compared to  transversions in it by way of base  substitutions base substitutions were  the most common type of mutation they  accounted for about half of them  single base insertions and single based  deletions each accounted for about a  quarter of the mutations  and the majority of them occurred at  homonucleotide tracts  as  illustrated by the examples here of a  uh 123a 124 which is an insertion after  oh sorry between 123 and 124  and delta 180 u which occurs in a run of  u residues uh around position 180.  so on dna and rna templates paul 2 is  more likely to commit transition errors  versus transversion errors and  preferentially commits indel errors at  homonucleotide tracts  um i think i'm going to skip this slide  here  for the interest of time  um  what you're looking at  is a diagram of the genome again  indicating  the mutations that we found  now to the outside of the genome  uh are our mutations  are the de novo mutations  and  inside our mutations found in progeny  populations i'll talk about that in a  minute  for the moment let's just talk about the  ones on the outside of the genome  sequence  so the the blue arrows are base  substitutions the red arrows indicate  insertion events and the brown triangles  indicate  the lesions  now the arrows indicate the most common  denoble mutations we found  a123 124 we actually found 19 times and  delta delta 180 u we actually found six  times  so  what you'll notice is that the mutations  appear to be clustered in certain  regions of the genome but what really  jumped out at us was that mutations were  mostly absent from the central conserved  region and the terminal conserved region  in fact  if we separately calculate the mutation  rate for those regions compared to the  remainder of the genome we find that  those  two conserved regions are transcribed  with an error rate about four-fold lower  than the remainder of the genome  so  the central conserved region and tc and  and the terminal conserve region  sequence conservation is the consequence  of both function based selection  and selection for greater paul ii  fidelity  now in a previous study  we deep sequenced progeny from pstvd  infected nicotiana benthamiana plants  and i won't go through this in great  detail but we looked for mutation hot  spots that were found in those variants  above a background threshold of 30  million uh  30 reads per million  and we identified  hot spots that were present in 10 unique  sequences per 